    {
      "id": "november_1912_a_bakery_in_moabit",
      "title": "November 1912 A Bakery In Moabit",
      "description": "Berlin, November 1912. Before sunrise, in a working-class street near Moabit, Hans the baker is already shaping dough in the warm back room of a small neighbourhood bakery, where a brick oven burns with coal and the flour settles over wooden tables. The shop is run by a baker’s household, supplying nearby factory workers, craftsmen, shop assistants, domestic servants, and families with dark rye loaves, mixed-grain bread, fresh Schrippen, and a few sweet pastries. Beyond the rain-streaked shop window, electric trams rattle through the grey morning, carts pass over wet cobblestones, and workers hurry toward their shifts. Inside, the oven doors creak, bread crusts crack, coins change hands, and warm loaves disappear into paper bags. Hans works among these ordinary sounds, while the woman serving at the counter knows many customers by face and remembers what they usually buy. More than a century later, their voices have vanished, but the little bakery remains imaginable through its bread, warmth, labour, and the rain falling outside its door.",
